DBMSolver: A Training-free Diffusion Bridge Samplerfor High-Quality Image-to-Image Translation Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2605.05889v1 [cs.CV] 07 May 2026 DBMSolver: A Training-free Diffusion Bridge Sampler for High-Quality Image-to-Image Translation Sankarshana Venugopal Email: sankarshana.v@gmail.com Mohammad Mostafavi Affiliation: Seoul National University Email: mostafavi.isfahani@gmail.com Jonghyun Choi † † thanks: Corresponding author. JC is with ECE, IPAI, ASRI in SNU. Email: jonghyunchoi@snu.ac.kr Abstract Diffusion-based image-to-image (I2I) translation excels in high-fidelity generation but suffers from slow sampling in state-of-the-art Diffusion Bridge Models (DBMs), often requiring dozens of function evaluations (NFEs). We introduce DBMSolver , a training-free sampler that exploits the semi-linear structure of DBM’s underlying SDE and ODE via exponential integrators, yielding highly-efficient 1 st 1^{	ext{st}} - and 2 nd 2^{	ext{nd}} -order solutions. This reduces NFEs by up to 5 × 5	imes while boosting quality (e.g., FID drops 53 % 53\% on DIODE at 20 NFEs vs. 2 nd 2^{	ext{nd}} -order baseline). Experiments on inpainting, stylization, and semantics-to-image tasks across resolutions up to 256 × 	imes 256 show DBMSolver sets new SOTA efficiency-quality tradeoffs, enabling real-world applicability. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/snumprlab/dbmsolver . Figure 1 : Few-step image synthesis (6 NFEs ↓ \downarrow ) with high-quality generated details (3.38 FID ↓ \downarrow ) on DIODE ( 256 × 256 ) ({256}	imes{256}) [ 37 ] . 1 Introduction Image-to-Image (I2I) Translation is a generative modeling paradigm that learns to map an input image to a target output. It encompasses tasks like image restoration, grayscale colorization, and inpainting of occluded or corrupted regions, as well as style transfer and semantic reinterpretation via cross-domain synthesis [ 36 , 14 , 7 ] . Recent diffusion-based works, as alternatives to traditional generative approaches such as GANs [ 45 , 16 ] , have brought significant advances in the synthesis of high-fidelity images [ 29 , 22 , 17 ] . Among them, [ 44 ] proposed Diffusion Bridge Models (DBMs), capable of performing I2I Translation by establishing a diffusion bridge that facilitates the translation from one arbitrary image distribution to another. While DBMs offer a theoretically elegant diffusion framework for I2I Translation, generating high-quality images using such diffusion-based models remains computationally expensive, as it requires numerous costly function evaluations (NFEs)–which we aim to tackle in this work without requiring any additional training overhead . DBMSolver: A Training-free Diffusion Bridge Sampler for High-Quality Image-to-Image Translation presents the fact that while Hybrid Heun [ 44 ] and DBIM [ 42 ] require numerous NFEs for coherent or high-quality outputs on DIODE [ 37 ] , our method (DBMSolver) achieves superior fidelity in just 6 NFEs, preserving intricate details like tree branches without artifacts. Unlike DBIM’s linear multistep methods [ 42 ] or audio-specific EI in Bridge-TTS [ 2 ] , DBMSolver derives closed-form solutions for VP/VE bridges, avoiding crude approximations and enabling FID gains even at low NFEs. 1.1 DBMSolver Overview We introduce DBMSolver : a training-free, highly efficient solver specifically designed to accelerate the DBM-based sampling process. Previous research on improving the sampling speeds explored either model distillation [ 9 , 40 , 8 ] , fine-tuning [ 6 , 9 ] , or re-training of an entire neural network [ 43 ] . In contrast, DBMSolver is a drop-in replacement for existing DBM sampling methods, avoiding the need for any architectural changes or extra training, thus enabling broad compatibility and immediate benefits. We devise DBMSolver by rigorously analyzing the underlying Stochastic and Ordinary Differential Equations (SDE and ODE) governing DBMs’ reverse-time diffusion process (Equations 2 and 5 ). Specifically, in Section 3.2 , we identify the inherent semi-linear structure of the SDE and leverage the Exponential Integrators (EI) [ 12 ] method to derive an efficient 1 st 1^{	ext{st}} -order solution. Next, in Section 3.3 , we analyze the ODE to show that it has a semi-linear structure as well, allowing us to formulate an exact solution that we efficiently approximate with a 2 nd 2^{	ext{nd}} -order solution. By combining the two ODE/SDE solutions, we devise a sampling procedure that drastically reduces the required NFEs while enhancing the image quality, as showcased in Section 3.4 . The key contributions of this work are as follows: • A novel, training-free bridge sampler : DBMSolver accelerates DBM sampling by 20x (e.g., 6 NFEs vs. 119 for Hybrid Heun) on conditional/unconditional I2I tasks, with zero retraining–unlike distillation [ 9 ] or fine-tuning [ 8 ] approaches. • Theoretically grounded : We provide analytical solutions for the Bridge SDE and ODE governing DBMs, grounded in diffusion bridge theory. • State-of-the-art performances on various I2I tasks : Through extensive experimentation on various I2I tasks and image resolutions, we show that DBMSolver consistently achieves state-of-the-art results, outperforming prior arts [ 42 , 20 , 39 ] in terms of generation quality and computational efficiency. 2 Preliminaries and Related Work Table 1 : Comparison of diffusion samplers . N2I methods assume Gaussian priors, invalid for DBMs’ arbitrary p T ​ ( 𝐱 ) p_{T}(\mathbf{x}) . Our DBMSolver uses Exponential Integrator (EI) [ 12 ] solutions for bridges, enabling Markovian, higher-order sampling. Sampling Method Assumption of Prior Distribution p T ​ ( 𝒙 ) p_{T}( oldsymbol{x}) Theoretically valid on Image-to-Image Translation Or